# Build context hygiene for services/tracking-api/Dockerfile.
#
# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .` and then `dotnet restore`, so without this every
# bin/ and obj/ from a local `dotnet build` is copied into the image build. That is
# not merely slow: obj/ carries absolute host paths and a project.assets.json
# pinned to the host's NuGet layout, so a restore inside the container can pick up
# stale state and fail, or worse, succeed against the wrong assets. CI never sees
# it (a fresh checkout has no bin/obj), which is exactly why it bites locally and
# nowhere else.
#
# There is no equivalent file for the root Dockerfile. That is an existing gap with
# the same cause, not an intentional difference -- worth closing separately rather
# than in a deploy-path change.
**/bin/
**/obj/

# Nothing in the image needs these, and excluding them keeps the context small
# enough that `docker build` does not spend its first seconds uploading history.
.git/
.gitignore
.vs/
.vscode/
*.user
*.suo

# The image publishes ClimateTracking.Api only; the test projects are restored by
# ClimateTracking.slnx during the build stage and never reach the runtime layer.
# They are kept in the context on purpose -- `dotnet restore ClimateTracking.slnx`
# resolves every project in the solution and fails if one is missing.
